# A Formal Examination of the Self-Fulfilling Prophecy of Ideals

## Why Ideal Processes Produce Their Own Falsification

---

**Abstract:** This article formally examines the proposition that the conditions of ideal process produce the means of their own falsification, and that logic—as an idealistic derivative—is unreliable as a foundation. Using GFF-1 as the analytical framework, we demonstrate that ideal processes (perfect fidelity, complete logic, absolute foundations) are self-falsifying: they generate the very conditions that reveal their incompleteness. This is not a failure of logic; it is the structural truth about all idealistic systems. The self-falsification of ideals is the mechanism by which humility is enforced, and by which the pursuit continues.

---

## 1. The Proposition

Let \( \mathcal{P}_{\text{self-falsify}} \) denote:

\[
\mathcal{P}_{\text{self-falsify}} := 
\begin{array}{l}
\text{The conditions of ideal process produce the means of its own falsification.} \\
\text{Logic is an idealistic derivative.} \\
\text{Logic is unreliable as a foundation.}
\end{array}
\]

We shall examine \( \mathcal{P}_{\text{self-falsify}} \) formally within GFF-1.

